# BrandKit

The page you open when a screen needs an element and the question is "how does
this go". Every decision rendered, with the code beside it.

**[DEV_URL_PATH]** <!-- e.g. "npm run dev, then localhost:5173/brandkit.html". Write "TBD until init-page runs" if the page does not exist yet. -->

## What it is for

Three things, in this order:

1. **Copy-paste.** Each block shows the rendered thing next to the code that
   produces it, with a button that copies it. If the code is not right there,
   the question comes back next time.
2. **Verification.** Contrast ratios are computed from what the browser
   actually rendered, not transcribed from `design.md`. If a token changes and
   nobody re-measures, the page says so.
3. **A single place to look.** The reasoning lives in `design.md` and
   `voice.md`; this shows the result. Where the two disagree, the docs win —
   see § Keeping it honest.

## Why it cannot reach production

The page is a **separate build entry, never declared in the build inputs** —
it is simply never built, which the build output confirms with no conditional
and no faith in tree-shaking. For the same reason its assets cannot live in a
folder the build copies wholesale (e.g. `public/`).

The app never imports from the page's directory: one such import would pull
its code into the production bundle without the build-output check seeing it.

## The tabs

| Tab | What it answers |
|---|---|
| **Foundations** | The mark, the typefaces, the type scale, spacing, radii — plus iconography and motion |
| **Colour** | The neutrals per theme, the accents/identities, ratios measured live |
| **Components** | [BLOCK_COUNT] blocks: [BLOCK_LIST] |
| **[DOMAIN_TAB_NAME]** | [DOMAIN_TAB_CONTENT] |
| **Layouts** | The shell and the screens, from the original mockups at real widths |
| **Voice** | Errors, empty states, destructive confirmation |
| **Accessibility** | The criteria, focus, target size, colour vision |

Controls sit above them and change everything at once ([CONTROLS_LIST]).
They are not tabs — the point is finding out whether a decision survives every
combination, not just the default.

A canonical block may be skipped only with a note here: "skipped: <reason>".

## How to add a block

```tsx
<Section
  title="What it is"
  note="Why it is that way — one or two sentences, not a paragraph."
>
  <Frame>{/* the specimen */}</Frame>
  <CodeBlock code={`/* the exact code to paste */`} />
</Section>
```

`wide` puts the note above rather than beside, for specimens that need the
whole line.

Four rules every block follows:

- **Official classes**, not raw tokens. What gets copied has to be what the
  app will write. Raw tokens appear only where the token *is* the content.
- **Chrome in the docs language, examples in [UI_LANG] — and literal.**
  Examples do not go through the i18n catalog: this page is a mockup, and
  coupling it to the catalog breaks the design system whenever a key renames.
  Per-block exceptions where the wiring itself is the point.
  **The licence is wording, not substance** — a specimen may phrase
  differently, it may not state a different rule.
- **Copy verbatim from the settled source.** Rewording a settled string makes
  this page a second, quietly different source.
- **Never colour alone.** A pass mark is a word, a state is a shape, a swatch
  carries its name.

## The mockups

Embedded mockups are **copies** of their source, never symlinks and never
redrawn in JSX — a redrawn copy drifts. Corrections made after copying live as
inline comments beside the changed line, so a re-copy that drops them shows in
the diff. Diff before every re-copy.

They keep their own stylesheets and do not follow the page controls: the
greyscale is how structure was decided.

The width must stay real: `<Wireframe>` scales with a CSS transform rather
than capping width, because a transform applies after layout — the document
inside still measures its true width and picks the right breakpoint.

## The specimen has to sit where the defect can show

A block that renders the right code can still be a passing test of nothing.
A component that shares its background with `Frame` proves nothing about the
boundary; a one-line specimen is a screenshot of the good case.

- Show the specimen in the state that breaks it, not the tidiest one.
- Put a control on the background it will actually meet.

## Keeping it honest

The BrandKit shows; it does not decide. When it disagrees with a doc, the doc
is right and the page is the bug.

| Change | Also update |
|---|---|
| A colour, type or spacing token | [design.md](design.md) first — this page reads from the tokens |
| A contrast ratio, or any token that moves one | The token stylesheet **and** the theme test that pins the ratios |
| Copy in an example | [voice.md](voice.md), and copy the wording back verbatim |
| A new block in `Components` | The block count in § The tabs — written out, not derived, so it goes stale silently |
| A string that also exists in the locale catalog | Copy it **verbatim**, punctuation included |
| A mockup source | Re-copy into the page's embedded copy — they are copies, not links; diff first |
| An accent/theme/identity | `PRODUCT.md` § Brand Commitments if it enumerates them; the token table in design.md |
